# Minimum Deletions to Make Array Sharp

## Approach: Greedy

### Theory
We traverse the array and compare the current element with the previously selected element using the XOR operator.

- If `nums[prev] ^ nums[index] == 0`, both elements are equal. We count a deletion and move to the next element.
- Otherwise, we keep the current element and update `prev` to its index.
- We repeat this process until all elements have been processed.

Since XOR of two equal numbers is zero, the condition helps identify duplicate values relative to the previously selected element.

## Code

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int minDeletions(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int index = 1;
        int prev = 0, count = 0;

        while (index < n) {
            while (index < n && (nums[prev] ^ nums[index]) == 0) {
                count++;
                index++;
            }

            prev = index;
            index++;
        }

        return count;
    }
};
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n)\), where \(n\) is the size of the array.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(1)\), as the algorithm uses only a constant amount of extra space.
